        "title": "refactor: use semver package for version ordering",
        "body": "Closes #105938\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nResolves a problem where update and plugin-install decisions relied on several independent version comparators, making prerelease, correction-release, and malformed-version ordering easier to implement inconsistently across workflows.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nUse `semver` 7.8.5 for standard parsing, comparison, coercion, and prerelease detection at the root and in plugins that import it directly. Keep OpenClaw's shipped numeric correction-release policy and legacy `x.y.z.beta.N` normalization as explicit layers around standard SemVer.\n\n## User Impact\n\nUsers and plugin operators get consistent version ordering across update checks, update channels, compatibility ranges, installs, updates, QA staging, and Codex app-server compatibility checks. No configuration or supported release shape changes.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Exact-head Blacksmith Testbox at `d26adc3cea4`: 457 focused tests passed across infra, config, plugin install/update, Codex, and QA surfaces ([run 29223834273](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29223834273)).\n- Requested broad `pnpm test src/infra src/plugins`: all infra and touched plugin tests passed; the earlier unrelated current-main guardrail drift was repaired upstream.\n- Exact-head `pnpm check:loc --base-ref origin/main`: passed. `pnpm check:changed` passed every preceding gate, then found unrelated QA-lab mock type drift in untouched `extensions/qa-lab/src/suite.test.ts`.\n- Remote `pnpm build`: passed.\n- Fresh xhigh autoreview: no accepted/actionable findings; patch correct at 0.98 confidence.\n- AI-assisted implementation and review; maintainer-authored change.\n",
